4. nixos-rebuild (for system configurations) → nix build

Old command: nixos-rebuild

On NixOS, nixos-rebuild is used for rebuilding the system configuration (e.g., packages, services, system settings).

New command: nix build (via flakes)

In flakes, the nix build command allows you to specify system configurations and deploy them declaratively, including nixosConfigurations from a flake.nix file.

Example (Old):

sudo nixos-rebuild switch


Example (New):

nix build .#nixosConfigurations.<hostname>.config.system.build.toplevel


The flake-based nix build command allows for rebuilding the NixOS configuration from a flake, specifying the system configuration output more directly.



nix-shell → nix develop

Old command: nix-shell

nix-shell was used to enter an environment with specific dependencies, often defined in a shell.nix file.

New command: nix develop

With flakes, the new nix develop command is used to enter a development
environment, often specified within a flake.nix. This is more in line with the
flake-based structure, offering greater reproducibility.


6. nix-store --gc → nix store gc

Old command: nix-store --gc

This command was used to perform garbage collection on the Nix store to free up space by removing unused store paths.

New command: nix store gc

Flakes don’t directly replace this command, but the general garbage collection process for unused store paths is now part of the newer nix store gc command.




nix-build for installable packages → nix run

Old command: nix-build was used to build specific derivations and installable packages from a default.nix.

New command: nix run

For running executable packages or apps directly from a flake, nix run replaces the traditional nix-build approach to building and executing packages.


nix-channel → Flake-based Channel Management

Old command: nix-channel

The nix-channel command was used for managing Nixpkgs channels (e.g., nixpkgs or custom channels) to update the state of available package sets.

New command: Flake-based channels are fully integrated into flakes, removing the need for nix-channel and channel updates. Flakes make channel management obsolete, as everything is tracked by the flake.nix file itself.
